}\uc1\pard\f0\fs24 PDP-8 Development Drive Setup Utility\f1  Guide\par
\par
\f2 Copyright \'a9 2016 \'bbCLA Systems\par
CLASystems@gmail.com\par
Last edit: 29-Aug-2016 cjl\par
\par
This document is a guide to the program logic used within the \ul PDP-8\par
\f0 Development Drive Setup\f1  Utility\ulnone  batch script file usually known as \f0\par
\i\f1 PDP-8_DRIVE_SET.BAT\i0 .  Certain advanced features are used that were first introduced in Windows XP, which is the earliest Windows system supported by the P?S Advanced SIMH Package [PASP].  Any additional changes to the program will be made to either correct minor cosmetic or logic errors.  The need for these changes may arise from specific incompatibility with various Windows configurations as the program has not been thoroughly tested with all of the supported Windows variants. Most notably, an advanced batch feature is used to work around a bug that presented while testing the package in Windows Vista 64-bit edition.  The implementation of the workaround is known to be compatible with all of the intended Windows systems through Windows 10; other problems will be fixed should they arise.\par
\par
The rest of this file consists of the actual batch script file punctuated as required with commentary indicated by the use of blue text such as \cf1 this\cf0 .  Perfunctory batch statements will not be commented on; it is assumed the reader has a basic understanding of Windows batch script programming.\par
\par
\fs29\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\fs20 @ECHO OFF\par
\fs24\par
\cf1\f1 Any batch statement that starts with the "@" character will not be displayed during execution.  In this case, the purpose of the statement is to turn off further output other than by use of ECHO and explicit display statements.\cf0\f0\par
\par
\fs20 Rem PDP-8 Development Drive Setup Utility                                27-Aug-2016\par
\par
Rem Copyright 1975-2016 CLA Systems.\fs24\par
\par
\cf1\f1 By using environment variables, the program version and creation date can be set in a central location, then used throughout the output as required.\par
\cf0\f0\par
\fs20 SET VERSION=1.2\par
\par
SET CREDATE=27-Aug-2016\fs24\par
\par
\cf1\f1 This statement creates a custom color screen for the batch screen using White characters on a blue background.\par
\cf0\f0\par
\fs20 COLOR 1f\fs24\page\cf1\f1 The overall size of the batch window can be adjusted to a custom value.  In this case, the usual 80 column window is used with an extended length of 37 lines.\par
\cf0\f0\par
\fs20 MODE CON: COLS=80 LINES=37\par
\par
Rem This program sets the development drive required by all PDP-8 development\par
Rem packages.  THe user environment variable PDP-8_DRIVE will be set to the desired\par
Rem drive letter [followed by ":"] which is used by all development packages as the\par
Rem drive to read components of the various packages from.\par
\par
Rem Output the title bar now.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The TITLE statement is used to modify the frame of the batch window. The program can change the frame text at any time during execution to produce various effects such as "thermometer" growing lines, etc.\par
\par
In this particular instance, the TITLE statement is very wide; it has been modified into two lines for documentation purposes only.  Depending on certain Windows parameters [usually set by default], the window frame text can extend to several hundred characters.  More recent versions of Windows batch allow very wide lines to support such features; no continuation characters are required.\par
\cf0\f0\fs20\par
Title PDP-8 Development Drive Setup Utility,       Version %VERSION%, Copyright (c) 2016, CLA Systems\par
\par
Rem First check if a valid file collection.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The dummy file \i DEVELOP\cf0\i0  \cf1 must be located in the current directory to indicate the batch script and required support files are completely present.\cf0\fs20\par
\f0\par
IF EXIST DEVELOP GOTO INSTALL\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO INSTALLATION FILES CORRUPT OR INCOMPLETE.  INSTALLATION ABORTED!\par
ECHO.\par
\par
:MISSCOM\par
\par
PAUSE\par
\par
EXIT\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The program requires the system Notepad editor which is used to display the associated help screen.\par
\cf0\f0\fs20\par
:INSTALL\par
\par
Rem First check if required system files are present.\par
\par
Rem Check if Notepad editor is installed.\par
\par
IF EXIST %SystemRoot%\\system32\\notepad.exe GOTO NTPDOK\par
\par
ECHO Notepad editor not installed!\par
\par
GOTO MISSCOM\page\cf1\f1\fs24 The program requires the command-line version of the registry editor to adjust the working parameters of the Notepad editor.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:NTPDOK\par
\par
Rem Check if system registry editor is installed.\par
\par
IF EXIST %SystemRoot%\\system32\\reg.exe GOTO REGOK\par
\par
ECHO Reg.exe program not installed!\par
\par
GOTO MISSCOM\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The program requires the freeware SLEEP timer program that creates specific time delays to ensure certain screen displays are seen by the user.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:REGOK\par
\par
Rem Check if sleep.exe file is present.\par
\par
IF EXIST sleep.exe GOTO SLEEPOK\par
\par
ECHO sleep.exe not found!\par
\par
GOTO MISSCOM\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The help screen text file must be present; it is used to provide some additional documentation to the user during the program execution.  The user must close the separate Notepad session window to continue the main program execution.  The help screen indicates this explicitly.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:SLEEPOK\par
\par
Rem Check if hlpscrn1.txt file is present.\par
\par
IF EXIST hlpscrn1.txt GOTO HLP1OK\par
\par
ECHO hlpscrn1.txt not found!\par
\par
GOTO MISSCOM\par
\par
\par
:HLP1OK\par
\par
Rem Check if we are in the correct directory.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 This is an advanced feature of batch for the express purpose of determining the exact path string for any file.  In this instance, the expected path is where the batch script and related files must be located.  The environment variable is set to the path string without the drive letter to allow proper testing below.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
FOR %%F IN (*.*) DO SET OURPATH=%%~PF\cf1\f1\fs24\page The environment variable string is compared to the expected path; this thwarts attempts to improperly use this utility.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
IF "%OURPATH%" == "\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\Links and Support Files\\PDP-8_SETUP\\" GOTO PATHOK\par
\par
ECHO Invalid installation directory!\par
\par
GOTO MISSCOM\par
\par
\par
:PATHOK\par
\par
Rem output opening screen.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 Assuming all components have been properly validated, the first display in the batch script window is outputted here.  Note the use of environment variables to output the current version and program creation date.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development Drive Setup Utility                   Version %VERSION%, %CREDATE%\par
\par
Rem ECHO.\par
ECHO Copyright (c) 2016, CLA Systems\par
ECHO CLASystems@gmail.com\par
\par
Rem Output starting date and time before (hopefully) setting the output drive.\par
\par
ECHO.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The current date and time are displayed; these are maintained by Windows and are accurate to at least the nearest two seconds.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
ECHO %DATE% %TIME%\par
ECHO.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The current logged-in user is displayed using the environment variable maintained by Windows.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
ECHO Current Profile: %USERNAME%\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO Gathering system information, please wait...\par
\par
Rem Output the gathering status on the title bar as well.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 All title frame considerations apply here as above; this allows for modification of the text in case the program might require extensive initialization time.  No actual change occurs as the delay is minimal on most Windows systems.  Future versions could implement a change of TITLE text followed later by another instance of a TITLE statement with the standard text as used below.  Practical testing shows that program execution is so fast, the user cannot discern any change in the TITLE frame text.  This is partially due to the design of Windows itself.  Future releases of this program may implement this feature should viable Windows configurations prove to be noticably slower; as of this writing, this program has not been tested with Windows 10.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
Title PDP-8 Development Drive Setup Utility,       Version %VERSION%, Copyright (c) 2016, CLA Systems\page Rem Setup Notepad editor for our help screen.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 Changes are made to the registry to customize the Notepad window for purposes of this utility.  The user preference settings are preserved and will be restored after the custom Notepad window closes.  Notepad preference settings apply to all uses of the Notepad editor; certain users prefer unusual settings.  As such, their usage will not be changed other than during the execution of this program.\line\line This program uses internal subroutines; this is a relatively new feature of batch; the only requirement is all subroutines exit via the very last line of the file; since multiple routines are present, the last line has its own label to effect proper exit for all cases.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
CALL :NTPDSET\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO Launching Notepad editor with Help Screen 1.\par
\par
Rem startup Notepad with our output file.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 Control is passed to the Notepad window until closed by the user.  This is accomplished using the START /WAIT command instead of simple program execution.\line\line An important feature of Notepad is the ability to pass the intended text file on the command-line; in this case the contents of the related help screen file are displayed.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
START /WAIT Notepad.exe hlpscrn1.txt\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 Program execution continues here when the separate Notepad window is manually closed by the user.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO Notepad editor closed.\par
ECHO.\par
\par
Rem Restore former Notepad editor settings.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The original user preference settings for Notepad will now be restored.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
CALL :NTPDREST\par
\par
:AGAIN\par
\par
Rem Check if PDP-8_DRIVE is already set before we got here.\par
\par
Rem First check if the variable is clear.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The primary purpose of this program is to setup the environment variable \i PDP-8_DRIVE\i0 .  First check if it is not present.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
IF "%PDP-8_DRIVE%"=="" GOTO NOTSET\page Rem PDP-8_DRIVE exists and must be validated.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The environment variable is nominally present; the actual contents must conform to our requirements of stating the correct drive letter.  An advanced feature of Windows batch is used to isolate the drive portion of the tested string [in this case the entirety of the environment variable string contents] for further testing.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
FOR %%F IN (%PDP-8_DRIVE%) DO SET DRV=%%~DF\par
\par
IF NOT "%PDP-8_DRIVE%"=="%DRV%" GOTO BADVAR\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 In certain specific circumstances, the current value of the environment variable may be nominally valid yet incorrect.  This can occur if the entire PASP drive is changed during a logged-in session; changes made to environment variables do not take effect until the current user session is logged off [if not a total reboot].\par
\cf0\f0\fs20\par
:MLOOP\par
\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development Drive already set.\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development packages will be located on drive %PDP-8_DRIVE%\par
ECHO.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The user interacts with the question regarding modification of the environment variable.  This advanced batch feature sets the stated environment variable to the string typed by the user which is terminated by pressing Enter [which is not included in the string].  In this case, only yes or no answers are acceptable in the form of either "Y" or "N".  Both upper and lower-case responses are allowed.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
SET /P YN=Modify? (y/n) \par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="Y" GOTO CHANGIT\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="y" GOTO CHANGIT\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="N" GOTO NOCHANGE\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="n" GOTO NOCHANGE\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 If the user types an unacceptable response, the program loops and asks the question repeatedly until a proper response is given.  The only other way to break out of the loop is to press Control-C which will offer to terminate the batch job.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
ECHO.\par
\par
GOTO MLOOP\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The user opted to preserve the present setting.  This is generally the proper course of action unless the unusual circumstances described above apply.\par
\par
\cf0\f0\fs20 :NOCHANGE\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development Drive setting will not be changed.\page\cf1\f1\fs24 All program exit points not associated with errors come here to exit after displaying the program output for an additional five seconds.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:GOODEXIT\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO Exiting...\par
\par
Rem Since this is not an error, the sleep time has been shortened.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The sleep timer program takes an in-line argument which is the delay time in seconds.  Certain versions of the program support decimal fractions; the freeware version included in the package only supports integers in the range of 0 through 99 [where 0 exits immediately without delay].\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
SLEEP 5\par
\par
EXIT\par
\par
\par
:CHANGIT\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The program continues here if the user opted to change the environment variable contents.\par
\cf0\f0\fs20\par
Rem Comes here to change existing source drive letter.\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development drive letter will be reset.\par
ECHO.\par
\par
GOTO NOTSET\par
\par
\par
:BADVAR\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The program comes here if the present contents of the environment variable string is not as expected.  The user is informed of this, including a statement of the present contents which will be changed.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
Rem Comes here if variable is corrupted or misformatted.\par
\par
ECHO The environment variable setting:\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO "PDP-8_DRIVE=%PDP-8_DRIVE%"\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO is incompatible with the PDP-8 Development Packages.\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO PDP-8_DRIVE must be reset to continue.\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO (Press control-C to cancel this program and preserve the current value.)\par
ECHO.\par
\par
PAUSE\par
\par
ECHO.\par
\par
Rem GOTO NOTSET\par
\page :NOTSET\par
\par
Rem Comes here if no prior source volume set.\par
\par
Rem Use our current drive as the source drive.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 An advanced feature of batch is used to obtain the drive letter of this package; this drive will be used as the basis of the environment variable string.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
FOR %%F IN (.) DO SET ICDRIVE=%%~DF\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The user is asked to continue with the drive letter determined to be suitable for use as the basis for the environment variable string.  As used in the early user input section above, only yes or no answers are acceptable.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:ALOOP\par
\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development packages will be located on drive %ICDRIVE%\par
ECHO.\par
\par
SET /P YN=Is this correct? (y/n) \par
\par
ECHO.\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="Y" GOTO ACCEPTIT\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="y" GOTO ACCEPTIT\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="N" GOTO NOACCEPT\par
\par
IF "%YN%"=="n" GOTO NOACCEPT\par
\par
GOTO ALOOP\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 If the program comes here, the user opted to abort the update.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:NOACCEPT\par
\par
ECHO Cancelled!\par
\par
GOTO BADEXIT\par
\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 If the program comes here, the user has agreed to the proposed change.  Ensure the full complement of support directories is present.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:ACCEPTIT\par
\par
Rem Check if this drive has the proper additional directories.\par
\par
IF NOT EXIST "%ICDRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\Links and Support Files\\PDP-8_SETUP\\" GOTO DRVINC\par
\par
IF NOT EXIST %ICDRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\ GOTO DRVINC\par
\par
Rem The drive seems to have the appropriate files to be used as the source drive.\par
\par
Rem Delete any previous setting.\page\cf1\f1\fs24 Check if the environment variable currently exists in the registry.  If so, it must be deleted before it can be updated.  All registry modifications are accomplished using the command-line version of the registry editor reg.exe.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
IF "%PDP-8_DRIVE%"=="" GOTO NODELETE\par
\par
REG DELETE "HKCU\\Environment" /v PDP-8_DRIVE /f > NUL:\par
\par
:NODELETE\par
\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Environment" /v PDP-8_DRIVE /t REG_SZ /d "%ICDRIVE%" /f > NUL:\par
\par
ECHO PDP-8 Development Drive Setup successful.\par
ECHO.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The environment variable was successfully updated.  Inform the user that the current logon session must be terminated completely to cause the changes to go into effect.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
ECHO Completely Log off %USERNAME% (switching users is not sufficient) to allow\par
ECHO the settings to update).\par
\par
GOTO GOODEXIT\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 All program exit situations arising from some problem will display the batch window for ten seconds to call special attention to the problem.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:BADEXIT\par
\par
Rem All of the error exit routines come here.\par
\par
ECHO.\par
ECHO Exiting...\par
\par
Rem This can be made longer if desired.\par
\par
SLEEP 10\par
\par
EXIT\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The program comes here if important components of the package are missing.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\f1\par
\f0 :DRVINC\par
\par
Rem Comes here is some vital files are missing.\par
\par
ECHO Selected source drive incomplete - Files missing, aborting . . .\par
\par
GOTO BADEXIT\page Rem Internal subroutines.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The NTPDSET routine is used to preserve all existing user preference settings of the Notepad editor; then custom values are set to create the desired properties of the separate Notepad Window used to display the help screen file.  Note: Certain lines of this routine have been\par
broken into two displayed lines for documentation purposes only.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:NTPDSET\par
\par
Rem Save whatever Notepad settings currently are.\par
\par
IF EXIST %TEMP%\\notepadbackup.reg DEL /Q %TEMP%\\notepadbackup.reg > NUL:\par
\par
REG EXPORT "HKCU\\SOFTWARE\\MICROSOFT\\NOTEPAD" %TEMP%\\notepadbackup.reg > NUL:\par
\par
Rem Customize Notepad for installation/guided help purposes.\par
\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfEscapement /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfOrientation /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfWeight /t REG_DWORD /d 400 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfItalic /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfUnderline /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfStrikeOut /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfCharSet /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfOutPrecision /t REG_DWORD /d 3 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfClipPrecision /t REG_DWORD /d 2 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfQuality /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfPitchAndFamily /t REG_DWORD /d 49 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iPointSize /t REG_DWORD /d 80 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v fWrap /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v StatusBar /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v fSaveWindowPositions /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v lfFaceName /t REG_SZ /d "Lucida Console" /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v szHeader /t REG_SZ /d "&f" /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v szTrailer /t REG_SZ /d "Page &p" /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iMarginTop /t REG_DWORD /d 1000 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iMarginBottom /t REG_DWORD /d 1000 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iMarginLeft /t REG_DWORD /d 750 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iMarginRight /t REG_DWORD /d 750 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v fMLE_is_broken /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iWindowPosX /t REG_DWORD /d 244 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iWindowPosY /t REG_DWORD /d 4 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iWindowPosDX /t REG_DWORD /d 538 /f > NUL:\par
REG ADD "HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Notepad" /v iWindowPosDY /t REG_DWORD /d 586 /f > NUL:\par
\par
GOTO THE_END\page\cf1\f1\fs24 The NTPDREST routine restores the original user preference settings for the Notepad editor.  Note: Certain lines of this routine have been\par
broken into two displayed lines for documentation purposes only.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:NTPDREST\par
\par
Rem Restore former notepad settings.\par
\par
Rem Redirecting both output streams to NUL: should not be required.  However, when\par
Rem running on Windows Vista, the successful report message outputs anyway unless both\par
Rem streams are stated explicitly.  While clearly a bug, this could also apply to other\par
Rem Windows releases.\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 The 1> and 2> notation is used to separate the normal output redirection from the error output redirection.  For unknown reasons, this eliminates the bug discovered when running the program in Windows Vista 64-bit edition; other releases may also exhibit this behavior.  The feature is supported in Windows XP and later Windows versions.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
REG IMPORT %TEMP%\\notepadbackup.reg 1> NUL: 2> NUL:\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 This is an example of the typical method used to suppress unwanted command execution output.  Note: Error output is normally impossible as this program creates the file to be deleted.  Any unwanted display is the result of some unknown Windows problem.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
DEL %TEMP%\\notepadbackup.reg > NUL:\par
\par
Rem GOTO THE_END\par
\par
\cf1\f1\fs24 This is the last line of the batch file.  All internal subroutines exit by reaching the last line of the file.  The previous line is a comment because NTPDREST is the last internal routine present.\cf0\f0\fs20\par
\par
:THE_END\par
\par
\f1\fs29\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\fs24\par
End-of-file]\cf1\f1\par
\cf0\f0\fs20\par
}
